From: Eduardo Fockink <54868856+EduardoFockink@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 18:50:42 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] drm: AQ_MGPU_CPU_COPY: support 10-bit (X/A*2101010)
 outputs for HDR

Reading a 2101010 buffer into CPU memory is a software conversion in the
NVIDIA driver: ~330 ms for a 5120x2160 frame, unusable. Instead a small
shader on the primary repacks each pixel's 32-bit word into an RGBA8
renderbuffer (only the scissored damaged rects), and that is read back on
the fast 8-bit path, byte-identical to the X*2101010 layout.

The dumb buffer is always allocated opaque (X*2101010): planes commonly
scan out [XA]RGB8888 and X*2101010 but not A*2101010 (Intel gen9 does
not). readRegionInto() now takes an eCpuCopyLayout instead of a GL format.

Verified word by word on the GTX 1050 Ti for both XBGR2101010 and
XRGB2101010. This makes HDR (bitdepth 10) usable on the Intel-driven
output.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
